'''bobolink''' (bob′ọ-lingk′), ''n.'' [Also ''boblink'', and earlier ''boblincoln'', ''boblincon'' (see above); an imitation of the bird’s note.] An American oscine passerine bird, of the family ''Icteridæ'' and subfamily ''Agelæinæ'', the ''Dolichonyx oryzivorus'', named from its hearty voluble song in
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Bobolink (''Dolichonyx oryzivorus'').}}}}
spring. {{smaller|The male is about 7½ inches long, black, with a buff nape, and much white or pale ash on the back and wings; the tail-feathers are very acute. The female is smaller, yellowish, darker above, and streaked. The}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|male wears the black livery only in the breeding season, and is only then in song. He molts in midsummer or in August, acquiring a plumage like that of the female. Both sexes are then known as ''reed-birds'' in the Middle States, as ''rice-birds'' in the Southern States, and as ''butter-birds'' in Jamaica. In the spring the male acquires his black and buff suit without molting any feathers: whence the correct popular notion, based, however, on erroneous premises, that the reed-birds turn into bobolinks in the spring. The bird is abundant in most of the United States, and is a regular migrant, breeding on the ground in meadows in the Northern States and Canada. In the fall, when fat and flocking in the marshes to feed upon wild oats (''Zizania''), it is much esteemed for the table. Also called ''bob-lincoln'', facetiously ''Robert of Lincoln'' (see ''bob-lincoln''), ''skunk-blackbird'', from its coloring, which resembles that of the skunk, and ''meadowink''.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The crack-brained ''bobolink'' courts his crazy mate,
Poised on a bulrush tipsy with his weight.</poem>
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Spring.}}}}

'''bob-sled''' (bob′sled), ''n.'' A sled consisting of a body resting on two short sleds called bobs, placed one behind the other. {{smaller|Bob-sleds are used for the transportation of timber, etc., and, when of lighter build for coasting, are also called ''double-runners'' or simply ''bobs''. [American.]}}
